<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <!--
    Engine wiring, the vertical slices, and the fluent builder (design 01
    section 3.1, ADR-0065's ratified name).

    THIS PROJECT TAKES ITS FRAMEWORK TYPES FROM A FrameworkReference AND NOT
    FROM PACKAGES, and that is a choice with a measured reason rather than a
    habit. `AddNamiIdentity` needs `IServiceCollection`, `IValidateOptions<T>`,
    `ValidateOnStart()` and `BindConfiguration()`. In a plain `Microsoft.NET.Sdk`
    library on net10.0 every one of those arrives as a separate NuGet package,
    which would mean four PackageVersion rows and four licence reads owed under
    ADR-0026 before a single line of Nami's own logic exists. Referencing the
    ASP.NET Core shared framework instead brings them with the runtime, which
    design 01's library table already records as ".NET and ASP.NET Core, Runtime
    and web host, MIT, ADR-0030". The engine does the same thing: read at the
    nuget.org flat container on 2026-08-08, `OpenIddict.Server.AspNetCore`
    7.5.0's nuspec declares a frameworkReferences group for net8.0, net9.0 and
    net10.0 and carries one package dependency, `OpenIddict.Server`.

    THE DEPENDENCY RULE STILL HOLDS AND IS NARROWER THAN IT LOOKS. Design 01
    section 3.1 says `Core` depends on "`Abstractions` plus the protocol
    engine" and "must not reference any adapter, database provider, or cloud
    SDK". A shared framework is none of those three. The architecture suite
    asserts the real rule against the compiled assembly, not against this file,
    for the reason `tests/CLAUDE.md` records: a test that reads a project file
    fails on a correct project.

    NO OpenIddict PackageReference YET, deliberately. `Directory.Packages.props`
    carries the bracket pins and `docs/DEPENDENCY-LICENSES.md` carries the
    licences, both landed with this project, but nothing here compiles against
    the engine so far. An unused reference is invisible to BOTH architecture
    facts, measured 2026-08-02 and recorded in `tests/CLAUDE.md`: with a package
    referenced and no code touching it, the type graph and the assembly
    reference table both pass. Adding the reference with the wiring that uses it
    is what makes the rule able to see it.

    TargetFrameworks reads the knob rather than naming a framework, because
    ADR-0030 parameter B multi-targets libraries and single-targets the host.
  -->

  <PropertyGroup>
    <TargetFrameworks>$(NamiLibraryTargetFrameworks)</TargetFrameworks>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <FrameworkReference Include="Microsoft.AspNetCore.App" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../Nami.Identity.Abstractions/Nami.Identity.Abstractions.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.CodeAnalysis.PublicApiAnalyzers" PrivateAssets="all" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
